One edit to packages/desktop-client/e2e/command-bar.test.ts and one line in the release note. No production code changes: CommandBar.tsx already renders the Radix title and description correctly and is not touched.

The console assertion is given a synchronisation point instead of a hope. The listener keeps pushing into `messages`, and additionally resolves a promise on the first message that matches. The test then awaits `Promise.race([firstDialogWarning, page.waitForTimeout(1000)])` before reading the array. On unfixed code the promise resolves as soon as the warning is delivered and the assertion fails fast, which is the regression this test exists for. On fixed code nothing matches, the 1000ms settle ends the wait, and the array is genuinely drained. The assertion stays ahead of the aria checks — the original comment's instinct was right, it just needed the drain to be true — and the aria assertions remain a second, independent line of defence, because Playwright falls back to `aria-label` when `aria-labelledby` dangles and so the role query alone would pass either way.

The regex is re-anchored to Radix's own wording, which the review transcribed verbatim from a real run: `DialogContent` requires a `DialogTitle` and `Missing Description or aria-describedby`. Both alternatives now require Radix's phrasing, so a log line that merely mentions `aria-describedby`, and cmdk's key-prop error, no longer match. The comment above the regex is rewritten to describe what it matches instead of claiming a narrowness it does not have.

The description assertion drops the English literal for the relationship the fix establishes: `aria-describedby` resolves to non-empty text, and to text that is not the title's. The title keeps its exact-text assertion, per the review, since `getByRole('combobox', { name: 'Command Bar' })` at line 64 already pins that string.

`referredText` takes `Locator` imported by name from `@playwright/test` and drops the `as string` cast, matching the suite.

The release note category becomes `Bugfix`.

